# Code fix policy

Applies to GitBot's Fix button, which reads this file directly, and to anyone
fixing a review finding by hand or with an agent.

## Change only what the finding names

Make the smallest edit that resolves the finding. Leave everything else in the
file alone: no reformatting, no renaming, no reordering, no tidying nearby code,
and no fixing a different problem you happen to notice. A second problem is a
second finding.

When a finding asks for a comment to be rewritten — into LiquidDoc, say — keep
what the comment said. A note about where a file lives or why a name must not
change is the kind of thing nobody can recover once it is gone.

## Match the file

Use the file's own indentation, quoting, naming, and the way it already does the
same thing elsewhere. Where the cited rule shows the pattern to use, use that
pattern.

## Keep behaviour, except for the defect

A fix must not change what a shopper sees or does beyond the defect itself: no
new copy, no removed attributes, no moved markup, no renamed classes that styles
or scripts may depend on. Do not delete code to make a finding go away unless
the finding is that the code should go.

Never change a `{% schema %}` setting id, a block type, or anything else that
saved merchant data points at. Renaming one silently drops what merchants have
configured, and nothing in the file shows it happened.

## When to decline

Decline, and say why, when:

- the finding is mistaken, or the code already does what it asks;
- the fix needs another file — a locale key, a snippet, a setting in another
  section — or anything you cannot see, such as merchant data or copy;
- the right fix is a judgement call between options, or a design decision.

Declining is a correct answer. A developer can still fix a finding by hand; they
cannot un-see a commit that changed something they did not ask about.

## Leave no trace but the fix

Do not add comments explaining the change. The commit says what was fixed and
why.
